  "worked_solution": "To find the two consecutive tenths between which $\\sqrt[3]{150}$ lies, we need to find two numbers with one decimal place whose cubes bracket $150$.\n\nFirst, let's establish between which two consecutive integers $\\sqrt[3]{150}$ lies.\nWe know that ${5}^{3}=125$ and ${6}^{3}=216$.\nSince $125<150<216$, we know that $5<\\sqrt[3]{150}<6$.\n\nNext, we can test decimals between $5$ and $6$ to narrow down the interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5.2}^{3} & =140.608 \\\\ {5.3}^{3} & =148.877 \\\\ {5.4}^{3} & =157.464\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $148.877<150<157.464$, we can write:\n\n$$\n{5.3}^{3}<150<{5.4}^{3}\n$$\n\nTaking the cube root of each part of the inequality gives:\n\n$$\n5.3<\\sqrt[3]{150}<5.4\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sqrt[3]{150}$ lies between $5.3$ and $5.4$.",
